  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ since it lies in the second quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$",
